Introduction to Next.js
Q1. What is Next.js?
Answer: Next.js is a React framework for building full-stack web applications.

React primarily provides the UI layer, whereas Next.js adds features such as routing, server-side rendering, server components, and backend endpoints.

For example, you can build an e-commerce application using Next.js with:
- Product listing pages.
- Dynamic product details.
- User authentication.
- Server-side data fetching.
- API endpoints.
- Search engine optimization.

Q2. Why do we need Next.js when React already exists?
React alone doesn't prescribe a complete application architecture.

Next.js provides several additional capabilities:
Feature	Purpose
File-based routing	Creates routes from your project structure
Server Components	Render components on the server by default
Client Components	Support browser interactivity and React hooks
Server-side rendering	Generate HTML on the server
Static rendering	Generate pages ahead of requests when appropriate
Route Handlers	Create backend HTTP endpoints
Server Actions	Execute server-side functions from supported client interactions
Metadata API	Configure page titles and SEO metadata
Image optimization	Optimize images through the Next.js Image component


Remember: Next.js is built on React. You still use React components, props, state, and hooks.
Q3. What is the difference between React and Next.js?
React	Next.js
UI library	React framework
Doesn't provide built-in file-based routing	Provides file-based routing
Client-side rendering is common in traditional React setups	Supports server and client rendering strategies
Requires additional tools for many application features	Integrates many application features
SEO depends on the rendering approach and setup	Provides built-in support for SEO metadata and server rendering


One important distinction: React itself supports server rendering APIs, too. Next.js provides an integrated framework around these capabilities.
Q4. What is the App Router?
The App Router is Next.js's routing system based on the app directory.

For example:
my-app/
├── app/
│   ├── layout.tsx
│   ├── page.tsx
│   ├── about/
│   │   └── page.tsx
│   └── products/
│       └── page.tsx
├── public/
├── package.json
└── tsconfig.json

This creates the following routes:
File	URL
app/page.tsx	/
app/about/page.tsx	/about
app/products/page.tsx	/products


Rule: A folder organizes a route segment, and a page.tsx file makes that route accessible.
